  "why": "`models/user.js` beside `models/user.model.ts` \u2014 the `x.model` / `x.service` naming convention. Module ids are dotted paths, so `ts:models/user.model.find` also *reads* as member `model` of `ts:models/user`. The third pass searched for the exported module by trimming at dots until it hit any readable CommonJS module, stopped at `ts:models/user`, found no export called `model`, and dropped every TypeScript edge into `user.model`: the call, the constructor, the method and the `extends`. The search must stop at the longest prefix that is a real module, and apply an export map only when that module has one. The fifth review pass: `lib/db.js` exports `{ config: loadConfig }` beside a sibling module `lib/db.config.ts`, so the call target `ts:lib/db.config` *is* a module id. The longest module prefix is then the whole target, and the call landed on the module. A target that is itself a module retries at the next shorter prefix that has an export map, which renames it to `loadConfig`.",
